-- Effects: effect declaration and well-known effect kinds
--
-- Each effect kind is a sort parameterized by what it ranges over:
--   sort Modify { sort T = ? }   — the effect MARKER; its argument names the
--                                  mutated RESOURCE, i.e. a PLACE, not a type.
--
-- A `Modify` target is a PLACE — a parameter (`Modify[c]`), the result binder
-- (`Modify[result]`), or a field path off one (`Modify[c.contents]`). It is
-- never a TYPE: kernel-language.md §5.6 reads `Env` as a partial map from
-- resource NAMES to terms, so `Modify[X]` says the operation may update
-- `Env(X)`, and a type in that slot names no slot at all. `Modify[T = c]` is
-- the same thing spelled with the binding named. Enforced at load by
-- `check_modify_targets` (kb/typing.rs); WI-20260823-39AD2 records why the
-- refusal and not a comparison.
--
-- The Modify marker carries only WHICH resource is mutated. Its runtime
-- operations live on a separate carrier sort `ModifyRuntime[T, V]` (below),
-- which adds the stored-VALUE type V — independent of the resource TYPE T,
-- which types the target place but is not itself the target.
--
-- Effect kinds are registered via `fact Effect[T = Kind[?]]` at namespace level,
-- or `provides Effect[T = Kind]` inside the sort that declares the kind — two
-- spellings of one provision, and the second is how `Time.Clock` and the three
-- `Console` kinds below register. CHECKED since WI-20260823-VM3YB: an effect row
-- naming an unregistered sort is a load error (`typing::check_effect_registration`).
-- Before that the registration was documented here and asked at no site, so a
-- misspelled label was a silent new effect and every one of these lines was inert.
-- The kernel checks effect declarations; interpretation is provided
-- by the realization layer (host-language, unsafe).
-- See kernel-language.md §5.5 (Effects).
--
-- Design principle: effects declare what is NOT visible from parameters.
-- Currently implemented:
--   Modify[target] — mutates a parameter (non-obvious from signature)
--   Error          — can fail with an untyped error
--   Error[type]    — can fail with a typed error
--
-- Runtime-handler semantics (Rust evaluator, M5):
--   A handler is registered per effect sort (`Modify`, `Error`, …), not
--   per instantiation. The `ModifyRuntime.get`/`set` operations route to the
--   default `Modify` handler, which holds one arena keyed by the target's
--   functor symbol — `set(store, v)` and `set(counter, v)` share the same
--   handler but live in separate slots within it. To give a specific resource
--   its own handler, replace the global Modify handler with a dispatcher that
--   switches on the target symbol.
--
-- AMBIENT RESOURCES — the spelling the note above describes. A NULLARY
-- CONSTRUCTOR is a constant of its sort, hence a value, hence a name for a slot:
--
--   sort CounterState
--     entity counter
--   end
--   operation write(n: Int64) -> Unit effects Modify[counter] = set(counter(), n)
--
-- No handle threaded through the caller; `Env(counter)` is the slot, and two
-- nullary constructors are two resources. A constructor that takes FIELDS is a
-- function and names no slot until applied; an EPONYMOUS one is its own sort
-- (§6.3) and stays a type. This is the one slot where a bare entity name is not a
-- type — kernel-language.md §5.6, WI-20260823-4GBQV.

namespace anthill.prelude

  -- Effect: meta-sort for effect registration.
  -- fact Effect[T = Modify[?]] means "Modify[target] for any target is a valid effect."
  -- The `[?]` reads well and is INERT: what a registration records is the KIND, its base
  -- sort, so `[T = Modify]` registers exactly the same thing. A label is judged on the
  -- sort it names, never on the arguments it applies (kernel-language.md §5.5).
  sort Effect {
    sort T = ?
  }

  -- Modifiable: marks a type as admitting a `Modify` over a PLACE OF THAT TYPE in
  -- effect rows — `fact Modifiable[T = Cell]` is what makes `Modify[c]` with
  -- `c: Cell` admissible. (`Modifiable`'s parameter is a TYPE; a `Modify` target
  -- never is — see the file header.)
  -- Per proposal 037 Rule 8 (bidirectional Modify↔handler correspondence): only
  -- types with a registered handler can be modified. A `fact Modifiable[T = X]` is
  -- the typing-level token; the runtime must back it with a registered handler
  -- before any `Modify` over a place of type X actually fires.
  --
  -- NOT YET ENFORCED — WI-20260823-39AD2 (second half): no site checks
  -- `Modifiable[typeof(target)]`, so `Modify[pattern]` on a `pattern: String`
  -- parameter loads clean.
  sort Modifiable {
    sort T = ?
  }

  -- Well-known effect kinds.
  -- Each sort defines its operations — the "pseudo API" that the effect provides.

  -- Modify[p]: the effect MARKER, keyed by the mutated resource — a PLACE.
  -- This is what effect rows carry (`effects Modify[c]` / `effects Modify[s]`);
  -- it says only WHICH resource is mutated, not what value it holds, and not
  -- its type. A TYPE in the slot (`Modify[T]`) is a load error — see the file
  -- header and kernel-language.md §5.6.
  sort Modify
    sort T = ?
  end

  -- ModifyRuntime[T, V]: the runtime carrier for the Modify-effect operations.
  -- It carries BOTH the resource-identity type T and the stored-value type V,
  -- which are INDEPENDENT (a `CounterState` resource may hold an `Int64`). Keeping
  -- them as separate parameters is what lets `get` return the value type rather
  -- than the resource type: the old `Modify { get(target: T) -> T }` folded both
  -- into one `T`, which only type-checked while arguments went unchecked and the
  -- expected return masked the contradiction — surfaced and rejected once WI-379
  -- made arguments authoritative.
  --
  -- V is currently inferred per call (from the value argument for `set`, from
  -- the expected return for `get`) and is NOT yet tied to the resource, so a
  -- wrong value-type read is not caught. Recording that tie on the resource
  -- (`sort CounterState { sort V = Int64 }` + `get(target: T) -> T.V`) is the
  -- sound destination — a structured-parameter / sort-carried-projection
  -- feature tracked in WI-383.
  sort ModifyRuntime
    import anthill.prelude.{Unit}
    sort T = ?
    sort V = ?
    operation get(target: T) -> V
    operation set(target: T, value: V) -> Unit effects Modify[target]
  end

  sort Error
    import anthill.prelude.{Nothing}
    sort T = ?
    operation raise(error: T) -> Nothing effects Error[T]
  end

  sort Suspension
  end

  sort Branch
    import anthill.prelude.{Nothing}
    operation fail() -> Nothing effects Branch
  end

  -- Payload for match-failure errors. Raised through `Error[MatchFailed]` by
  -- the evaluator when a `match` expression has no matching arm. The
  -- exhaustiveness-checked typer removes `Error[MatchFailed]` from a match's
  -- inferred effect row when all arms are covered.
  sort MatchFailed
    import anthill.reflect.{NodeOccurrence, Term}
    entity match_failed(occurrence: NodeOccurrence, scrutinee: Term)
  end

  -- Payload for division-by-zero errors. Carried by `Error[DivisionByZero]` on
  -- the partial integer operations (`Int64.div` / `mod` / `rem` / `divExact`)
  -- and `Field.div` / `Field.recip` (WI-066). Carries `op` — the name of the
  -- operation that divided by zero (e.g. `"Int64.div"`) — so a handler that
  -- catches `Error[DivisionByZero]` can report which op failed. WI-467 routes
  -- the runtime raise through the Error handler (`interp.raise_error`), so an
  -- installed `Error[...]` handler actually catches it; unhandled, it surfaces
  -- as `EvalError::Raised` carrying this payload.
  sort DivisionByZero
    import anthill.prelude.{String}
    entity division_by_zero(op: String)
  end

  -- Payload for head-of-empty errors. Carried by `Error[EmptyStream]` on the
  -- partial `Stream.head` (WI-567) — the ergonomic bare-`T` head, guarded by
  -- `isEmpty(s)`. A nullary marker — the stream is empty by definition, so there
  -- is nothing to carry (cf. `DivisionByZero`). This sort is the TYPE-level
  -- witness the typer threads through callers' rows. NOTE: unlike `DivisionByZero`
  -- (a native builtin the evaluator raises as `EvalError::DivisionByZero`), the
  -- eval-side raise is ordinary surface code (WI-818, delivering the WI-567
  -- follow-on): `Stream.head`/`tail`'s default bodies — and `List.head`'s
  -- override — `Error.raise(empty_stream)` on the empty case, so unhandled it
  -- surfaces as `EvalError::Raised` carrying this payload.
  sort EmptyStream
    entity empty_stream
  end

  -- Payload for a FLOUNDERED relation drain (WI-737). Carried by `Error` on the
  -- Relation face (proposal 052) when materializing an answer that the search
  -- never DECIDED: a solution with a non-empty residual — goals that delayed
  -- waiting on variables which never got bound. `goals` are those undischarged
  -- goals, so a handler can report WHICH goal floundered.
  --
  -- Why this raises rather than yields, and why only here. A `Relation[T]`
  -- promises rows of `T`; `T` has no room for a third "undecided" outcome, so
  -- materializing a floundered answer would either put a logic VARIABLE in a
  -- column the schema types (`Relation[(x: Int64, …)]` yielding `x = ?_1232`) or
  -- read a residual as positive membership. `E ⊇ {Error}` on every relation
  -- already, so this rides a channel the type declares — no signature changes.
  --
  -- THE SPLIT (WI-519): the RAW reflect / LogicalStream face keeps undecidedness
  -- as DATA — `execute` yields `undecided(subst, residual)` and NEVER raises it,
  -- so WI-010's self-hosted resolver can inspect which goals stayed pending. The
  -- TYPED Relation face raises, because a typed row cannot carry that third
  -- outcome. Same fact, two faces; see `Interpreter::make_solution_value` (the
  -- data face) vs `materialize_solution` (this one) in `eval/mod.rs`.
  sort RelationFloundered
    import anthill.prelude.{List}
    import anthill.reflect.{Term}
    entity relation_floundered(goals: List[T = Term])
  end

  -- Register as effects
  fact Effect[T = Modify[?]]
  fact Effect[T = Error[?]]
  fact Effect[T = Suspension]
  fact Effect[T = Branch]
end

-- Effect relationships:
-- Modify implies the ability to read
-- (Read is not a separate effect kind — it's implied by Modify
--  and operations that only read should take immutable parameters)
